  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...In the wake of today's cold front, an \nanomalously deep upper trough is forecast to develop off the \nMid-Atlantic Coast to the east of a dome of hot and humid air \nover the Central U.S. This will setup an elevated thermal \ngradient oriented NW to SE across the Mid-Atlantic and \nCarolinas. Increasing moisture and instability along this \nboundary will combine with various shortwaves dropping SE along \nthe periphery of Central U.S. ridge, supporting scattered \nclusters of thunderstorms from Sunday night through Monday \nnight. Southerly low-level flow beneath NW flow aloft will \nsupport increasing deep layer shear on the order of 30-40kt. \nThere is some uncertainty regarding how much instability will \ndevelop locally along the boundary. However, the increased deep \nlayer shear and a favorable NW flow convective pattern appears \nsupportive of a few rounds of strong to potentially severe \nconvection. Of note, some of this convection may even occur at \nnight thanks to a continued influx of moisture and instability \ninto the thermal gradient. \n\nWhile instability may be on the lower side for this time of \nyear, ECMWF's EFI guidance suggests the presence of stronger \ndeep layer shear may setup a notable shear/instability combo for\nthis time of year. While this doesn't guarantee severe weather \nlocally, it's a scenario that bears watching. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Anomalous upper troughing is forecast to remain\nin place across the Mid-Atlantic and Carolinas through at least\nthe middle of next week. Within this regime, a convectively \nactive NW flow pattern appears likely, and this is supported by \ndeterministic, analog, and machine learning guidance. The signal\nis the strongest on Tuesday, and this is the day that the Storm\nPrediction Center has outlooked a portion of our area in a \nSlight Risk of severe thunderstorms. Synoptically, the overlap \nof building instability and notably strong deep layer shear for \nthis time of year certainly appears favorable for severe \nthunderstorm development.\n\nIn addition to the potential convective hazards, there will \nalso be a risk of dangerous heat. This risk also looks to be \nfocused on Tuesday when dewpoints and air temperatures are \nforecast to be the highest, with heat indices of 100-105 \ndegrees.",
